[Music]  thankfully one of the biggest scams in  the American tax code is finally under  attack in the House version of  Republican tax reform  it's the mortgage interest deduction  which currently lets homeowners deduct  interest paid on mortgages of up to 1  million dollars for two houses  [Music]  ever since owning a home has been a  central tenet of the American Dream  since the end of World War two in the  rise of suburbia it's been a given that  deducting mortgage interest from your  taxes is as American as apple pie  the house plan would limit filers to  deducting interest on the first five  hundred thousand dollars of a mortgage  on just one house sending a blind panic  through wealthy homeowners Realtors and  the building trades all of whom are  terrified that a government subsidy is  being yanked away from them we are very  concerned with the limitation on the  mortgage interest deduction and it's  really limited into what's the real  problem with the House bill is that it  doesn't go far enough  we should scrap the mortgage interest  deduction all together and let housing  prices reflect real market values the  mortgage interest deduction is typically  justified by claiming that it lets  people especially vaguely defined  middle-class people afford homes but it  also increases the price of housing by  making it artificially cheap to borrow  money meaning homebuyers are willing to  borrow more England Canada and Australia  don't let their tax payers deduct their  mortgage interest and they all have  higher rates of homeownership than the  United States the mortgage interest  deduction disproportionately benefits  the wealthiest Americans who soak up  almost all of the seventy billion  dollars a year it costs in foregone  revenue each year the tax bill both the  House and the Senate versions both of  them leave alone for the most part the  mortgage interest deduction which is one  of the most ridiculous parts of the tax  code recent foundations director of 